<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danton's Death</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Dantons Tod</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Danton's Death is a play by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-buchner">Georg Büchner</a>, written in 1835 while he was hiding from the Hessian police after publishing a revolutionary pamphlet. It portrays the last days of the French revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-danton">Georges Danton</a> before his execution under Robespierre's Terror, using speeches from historical sources. It was first staged in 1902 and is now a classic of German drama.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dantons-death.jpg" alt="Danton's Death" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Katja Rehfeld · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-Z0420-027,_Berlin,_%22Dantons_Tod%22.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>It was Büchner's first play. He began his research late in 1834 and wrote a first complete version in five weeks, from mid-January to mid-February 1835, in constant fear of arrest. Karl Gutzkow published a version that year in the Literatur-Blatt of Eduard Duller's Phönix, and a book edition followed from Johann David Sauerländer's Phönix-Verlag with the subtitle Dramatic Scenes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s Reign of Terror, chosen to appease the censor. Heavily cut, its politics softened with sexual innuendo, it was the only one of Büchner's plays published in his lifetime.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The play is set in the lull between the first and second terrors. Danton is shown as a man at ease, given to wine, gaming and women, who respects the Revolution's successes but doubts its further aims, set against the poverty, begging and prostitution of the streets. His friends urge him to fight or flee Robespierre's supporters, but he believes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> will not dare to act against him, and he confides to his wife Julie his guilt over the September Massacres. Robespierre appears as a man of social conscience admired as the incorruptible, whom others call a terrorist.</p><p>Before the tribunal Danton at first wins the public's sympathy, but at its second sitting the people turn against him over his way of life. He and his friends are condemned, and he and Camille Desmoulins talk of life and death. Julie poisons herself at home, and when Lucile Desmoulins sees her husband mount the scaffold she cries Long live the king, ensuring her own death sentence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>For a long time no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> dared to stage the play. Its premiere came on 5 January 1902 at the Belle-Alliance-Theater in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in a production of the Verein Neue Freie Volksbühne. Because it draws on many historical sources and quotes original political speeches at length, the 20th century saw it as a forerunner of documentary theatre. It was the basis of Dimitri Buchowetzki's silent film Danton of 1921, with Emil Jannings, and of Gottfried von Einem's opera Dantons Tod, first performed at the Salzburg Festival in 1947. Howard Brenton adapted it for the English stage in 1982 and again in 2010 for the Royal National Theatre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Danton's Death” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Georg Büchner, born near Darmstadt, wrote <b>Danton's Death</b> and Woyzeck before his early death in exile in 1837.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
